"class":137},[131,1315,1316],{"class":148}," posts, unnest(tags) ",[131,1318,1319],{"class":137},"AS",[131,1321,1322],{"class":148}," tag;\n",[300,1324,1326,1329],{"id":1325},"unnest-array-to-rows",[18,1327,1328],{},"unnest"," — array to rows",[436,1331,1332],{"language":121},[123,1333,1335],{"className":125,"code":1334,"language":121,"meta":127,"style":127},"-- Each post's tags as separate rows\nSELECT id, unnest(tags) AS tag FROM posts;\n\n-- Aggregate tags back into an array\nSELECT id, array_agg(tag) FROM (SELECT id, unnest(tags) AS tag FROM posts) t GROUP BY id;\n\n-- Turn a comma-separated string into an array\nSELECT string_to_array('a,b,c', ',');   -- {a,b,c}\nSELECT array_to_string(ARRAY['a','b','c'], ',');   -- a,b,c\n",[18,1336,1337,1342,1359,1363,1368,1398,1402,1407,1428],{"__ignoreMap":127},[131,1338,1339],{"class":133,"line":134},[131,1340,1341],{"class":192},"-- Each post's tags as separate rows\n",[131,1343,1344,1346,1349,1351,1354,1356],{"class":133,"line":152},[131,1345,239],{"class":137},[131,1347,1348],{"class":148}," id, unnest(tags) ",[131,1350,1319],{"class":137},[131,1352,1353],{"class":148}," tag ",[131,1355,276],{"class":137},[131,1357,1358],{"class":148}," posts;\n",[131,1360,1361],{"class":133,"line":167},[131,1362,186],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":185},[131,1364,1365],{"class":133,"line":176},[131,1366,1367],{"class":192},"-- Aggregate tags back into an array\n",[131,1369,1370,1372,1375,1377,1379,1381,1383,1385,1387,1389,1392,1395],{"class":133,"line":182},[131,1371,239],{"class":137},[131,1373,1374],{"class":148}," id, array_agg(tag) ",[131,1376,276],{"class":137},[131,1378,208],{"class":148},[131,1380,239],{"class":137},[131,1382,1348],{"class":148},[131,1384,1319],{"class":137},[131,1386,1353],{"class":148},[131,1388,276],{"class":137},[131,1390,1391],{"class":148}," posts) t ",[131,1393,1394],{"class":137},"GROUP BY",[131,1396,1397],{"class":148}," id;\n",[131,1399,1400],{"class":133,"line":189},[131,1401,186],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":185},[131,1403,1404],{"class":133,"line":196},[131,1405,1406],{"class":192},"-- Turn a comma-separated string into an array\n",[131,1408,1409,1411,1414,1417,1419,1422,1425],{"class":133,"line":217},[131,1410,239],{"class":137},[131,1412,1413],{"class":148}," string_to_array(",[131,1415,1416],{"class":211},"'a,b,c'",[131,1418,248],{"class":148},[131,1420,1421],{"class":211},"','",[131,1423,1424],{"class":148},");   ",[131,1426,1427],{"class":192},"-- {a,b,c}\n",[131,1429,1430,1432,1435,1438,1441,1443,1445],{"class":133,"line":222},[131,1431,239],{"class":137},[131,1433,1434],{"class":148}," array_to_string(",[131,1436,1437],{"class":137},"ARRAY",[131,1439,1440],{"class":148},"['a','b','c'], ",[131,1442,1421],{"class":211},[131,1444,1424],{"class":148},[131,1446,1447],{"class":192},"-- a,b,c\n",[14,1449,1450,1452],{},[18,1451,1328],{}," is the bridge between array columns and relational operations — it expands arrays for joins, aggregations, and filtering.",[23,1454,1456],{"id":1455},"when-to-use-jsonb-vs-arrays-vs-normalized-tables","When to Use JSONB vs Arrays vs Normalized Tables",[1458,1459,1460,1467,1473],"ul",{},[1461,1462,1463,1466],"li",{},[105,1464,1465],{},"Normalized tables"," — when the nested data is structured, queried independently, or joined often. Relations are still PostgreSQL's strongest suit.",[1461,1468,1469,1472],{},[105,1470,1471],{},"Arrays"," — for homogeneous lists (tags, categories) that are always read\u002Fwritten together and rarely filtered individually. Smaller and simpler than a join table for simple many-value attributes.",[1461,1474,1475,1477],{},[105,1476,20],{}," — for variable-structure data (event payloads, API responses, user settings) where the schema evolves or varies per row, and you query by containment on some keys.",[14,1479,1480],{},"A common anti-pattern: stuffing everything into a JSONB column (\"schemaless\") and then needing to query\u002Ffilter on nested fields — at which point normalizing would have been better. Use JSONB for genuine semi-structured data; normalize for structured relationships you query relationally.",[23,1482,1484],{"id":1483},"tips-tricks","💡 Tips & Tricks",[1458,1486,1487,1503,1512,1535,1547],{},[1461,1488,1489,1492,1493,1496,1497,1500,1501,331],{},[105,1490,1491],{},"Idiom",": prefer ",[18,1494,1495],{},"payload @> '{\"key\": \"value\"}'"," (containment) over ",[18,1498,1499],{},"payload->>'key' = 'value'"," (extraction + comparison) — containment can use a full GIN index on the JSONB column, while extraction+comparison needs a per-path expression index. Design queries around ",[18,1502,381],{},[1461,1504,1505,1507,1508,1511],{},[105,1506,1491],{},": use ",[18,1509,1510],{},"jsonb_build_object(...)"," to construct JSONB from columns in a query — cleaner and less error-prone than string concatenation, and it handles quoting\u002Fescaping correctly.",[1461,1513,1514,1517,1518,1521,1522,248,1524,248,1526,248,1529,1531,1532,1534],{},[105,1515,1516],{},"Performance",": a ",[105,1519,1520],{},"GIN index on a JSONB column"," is the single most powerful JSONB optimization — it accelerates ",[18,1523,381],{},[18,1525,404],{},[18,1527,1528],{},"?|",[18,1530,425],{}," for any path. For a hot subset, use a partial GIN (",[18,1533,615],{},") to shrink the index.",[1461,1536,1537,1507,1539,1542,1543,1546],{},[105,1538,1491],{},[18,1540,1541],{},"unnest(array_col)"," to expand an array column into rows for joins\u002Faggregation, and ",[18,1544,1545],{},"array_agg(col)"," to fold rows back into an array — the two are inverses, and together let you move between array and relational form as needed.",[1461,1548,1549,1552,1553,1556,1557,1559,1560,1563,1564,710,1566,1568,1569,710,1571,1573,1574,1576],{},[105,1550,1551],{},"Portability",": JSONB is PostgreSQL-specific (MySQL has ",[18,1554,1555],{},"JSON",", SQLite stores JSON as ",[18,1558,1020],{}," with ",[18,1561,1562],{},"json_extract","). The ",[18,1565,324],{},[18,1567,338],{},"operators are PostgreSQL (MySQL uses ",[18,1570,324],{},[18,1572,338],{}," too but with some differences; SQLite uses ",[18,1575,1562],{},"). JSON querying is one of the least portable areas — write per-engine.",[23,1578,1580],{"id":1579},"️-edge-cases-gotchas","⚠️ Edge Cases & Gotchas",[1458,1582,1583,1602,1622,1649,1665,1684,1690,1706,1724,1737,1757],{},[1461,1584,1585,1590,1591,1594,1595,1598,1599,1601],{},[105,1586,1587,1589],{},[18,1588,87],{}," deduplicates keys",": ",[18,1592,1593],{},"jsonb '{\"a\": 1, \"a\": 2}'"," keeps only the last value (",[18,1596,1597],{},"{\"a\": 2}","). ",[18,1600,65],{}," preserves both. Usually fine, but surprising if you expected to see duplicates.",[1461,1603,1604,1590,1609,1612,1613,1616,1617,1619,1620,331],{},[105,1605,1606,1608],{},[18,1607,87],{}," doesn't preserve key order",[18,1610,1611],{},"jsonb '{\"b\": 1, \"a\": 2}'"," may display as ",[18,1614,1615],{},"{\"a\": 2, \"b\": 1}"," (keys are sorted). ",[18,1618,65],{}," preserves input order. If order matters (rare), use ",[18,1621,65],{},[1461,1623,1624,1590,1632,1634,1635,1638,1639,1642,1643,1645,1646,1648],{},[105,1625,1626,1628,1629,1631],{},[18,1627,324],{}," returns JSONB, ",[18,1630,338],{}," returns text",[18,1633,330],{}," is JSONB (you can chain ",[18,1636,1637],{},"->'id'","); ",[18,1640,1641],{},"payload->>'user'"," is text (you can't chain). Use ",[18,1644,324],{}," for nested traversal, ",[18,1647,338],{}," for the final scalar.",[1461,1650,1651,1590,1654,1657,1658,1661,1662,331],{},[105,1652,1653],{},"Numbers in JSONB",[18,1655,1656],{},"payload->>'amount'"," returns text — ",[18,1659,1660],{},"WHERE payload->>'amount' > 100"," does string comparison, not numeric. Cast: ",[18,1663,1664],{},"WHERE (payload->>'amount')::numeric > 100",[1461,1666,1667,1590,1672,1675,1676,1679,1680,1683],{},[105,1668,1669,1671],{},[18,1670,381],{}," requires matching types",[18,1673,1674],{},"payload @> '{\"id\": 42}'"," matches only if ",[18,1677,1678],{},"id"," is stored as a number. If it's stored as a string ",[18,1681,1682],{},"\"42\"",", the containment fails. JSONB preserves the input type.",[1461,1685,1686,1689],{},[105,1687,1688],{},"GIN index size",": a full GIN index on a large JSONB column can be huge (indexes every key\u002Fvalue). For large tables, prefer expression indexes on the specific paths you query.",[1461,1691,1692,1702,1703,331],{},[105,1693,1694,1697,1698,1701],{},[18,1695,1696],{},"jsonb_set"," creates the key only if ",[18,1699,1700],{},"create_if_missing"," is true"," (the 4th arg, default true). To set only existing keys, pass ",[18,1704,1705],{},"false",[1461,1707,1708,1590,1713,1716,1717,1720,1721,1723],{},[105,1709,1710,1712],{},[18,1711,902],{}," merges at the top level only",[18,1714,1715],{},"payload || '{\"user\": {\"name\": \"Bob\"}}'"," replaces the entire ",[18,1718,1719],{},"user"," object, it doesn't deep-merge. For deep merge, use ",[18,1722,1696],{}," on the specific path.",[1461,1725,1726,1590,1729,1732,1733,1736],{},[105,1727,1728],{},"Arrays are 1-indexed",[18,1730,1731],{},"arr[1]"," is the first element, ",[18,1734,1735],{},"arr[0]"," is NULL (not an error). Coming from a 0-indexed language, this is a common off-by-one.",[1461,1738,1739,1590,1745,1748,1749,1752,1753,1756],{},[105,1740,1741,1744],{},[18,1742,1743],{},"array_length(arr, 1)"," is NULL for empty arrays",[18,1746,1747],{},"array_length('{}', 1)"," = NULL, not 0. Use ",[18,1750,1751],{},"coalesce(array_length(arr, 1), 0)"," for 0, or ",[18,1754,1755],{},"cardinality(arr)"," (returns 0 for empty).",[1461,1758,1759,1590,1767,1770,1771,1774,1775,1770,1778,1781,1782,1784,1785,1787,1788,1791],{},[105,1760,1761,1764,1765],{},[18,1762,1763],{},"ANY"," vs ",[18,1766,381],{},[18,1768,1769],{},"'x' = ANY(arr)"," checks if ",[18,1772,1773],{},"x"," is an element; ",[18,1776,1777],{},"arr @> array['x']",[18,1779,1780],{},"arr"," contains ",[18,1783,1773],{},". They're equivalent for single-value checks, but ",[18,1786,381],{}," can check multiple values at once (",[18,1789,1790],{},"arr @> array['x', 'y']"," = \"contains both\") and uses a GIN index.",[23,1793,1795],{"id":1794},"spot-the-bug","🧠 Spot the Bug",[14,1797,1798,1799,1802],{},"A developer indexes the ",[18,1800,1801],{},"type"," field of a JSONB column and is surprised the query doesn't use the index:",[436,1804,1805],{"language":121},[123,1806,1808],{"className":125,"code":1807,"language":121,"meta":127,"style":127},"CREATE INDEX events_type_idx ON events ((payload->>'type'));\n\nSELECT * FROM events WHERE payload->>'type' = 'click';\n-- EXPLAIN shows a seq scan, not an index scan\n",[18,1809,1810,1829,1833,1857],{"__ignoreMap":127},[131,1811,1812,1814,1816,1818,1820,1823,1825,1827],{"class":133,"line":134},[131,1813,138],{"class":137},[131,1815,642],{"class":137},[131,1817,733],{"class":144},[131,1819,648],{"class":137},[131,1821,1822],{"class":148}," events ((payload",[131,1824,338],{"class":137},[131,1826,245],{"class":211},[131,1828,745],{"class":148},[131,1830,1831],{"class":133,"line":152},[131,1832,186],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":185},[131,1834,1835,1837,1839,1841,1843,1845,1847,1849,1851,1853,1855],{"class":133,"line":167},[131,1836,239],{"class":137},[131,1838,560],{"class":137},[131,1840,461],{"class":137},[131,1842,565],{"class":148},[131,1844,282],{"class":137},[131,1846,454],{"class":148},[131,1848,338],{"class":137},[131,1850,245],{"class":211},[131,1852,775],{"class":137},[131,1854,778],{"class":211},[131,1856,294],{"class":148},[131,1858,1859],{"class":133,"line":176},[131,1860,1861],{"class":192},"-- EXPLAIN shows a seq scan, not an index scan\n",[14,1863,1864],{},"What's a likely cause?",[1866,1867,1868,1872,1895,1898,1949,1964,1967,1997],"details",{},[1869,1870,1871],"summary",{},"Answer",[14,1873,1874,1875,1878,1879,1882,1883,1887,1888,1891,1892,1894],{},"The expression index is on ",[18,1876,1877],{},"(payload->>'type')",", and the query filters on ",[18,1880,1881],{},"payload->>'type' = 'click'"," — the expressions match, so the index ",[1884,1885,1886],"em",{},"should"," be usable. The likely cause is that the ",[105,1889,1890],{},"planner chose a seq scan because it's cheaper"," — if the table is small, or if ",[18,1893,251],{}," events are most of the table, scanning is faster than index lookup + heap fetch.",[14,1896,1897],{},"To confirm, force the index and compare:",[123,1899,1901],{"className":125,"code":1900,"language":121,"meta":127,"style":127},"SET enable_seqscan = off;\nEXPLAIN SELECT * FROM events WHERE payload->>'type' = 'click';\n-- Now it should show an index scan — if it's still slow, the index isn't the issue.\n",[18,1902,1903,1917,1944],{"__ignoreMap":127},[131,1904,1905,1907,1910,1912,1915],{"class":133,"line":134},[131,1906,858],{"class":137},[131,1908,1909],{"class":148}," enable_seqscan ",[131,1911,288],{"class":137},[131,1913,1914],{"class":137}," off",[131,1916,294],{"class":148},[131,1918,1919,1922,1924,1926,1928,1930,1932,1934,1936,1938,1940,1942],{"class":133,"line":152},[131,1920,1921],{"class":148},"EXPLAIN ",[131,1923,239],{"class":137},[131,1925,560],{"class":137},[131,1927,461],{"class":137},[131,1929,565],{"class":148},[131,1931,282],{"class":137},[131,1933,454],{"class":148},[131,1935,338],{"class":137},[131,1937,245],{"class":211},[131,1939,775],{"class":137},[131,1941,778],{"class":211},[131,1943,294],{"class":148},[131,1945,1946],{"class":133,"line":167},[131,1947,1948],{"class":192},"-- Now it should show an index scan — if it's still slow, the index isn't the issue.\n",[14,1950,1951,1952,1955,1956,1959,1960,1963],{},"::\nIf forcing the index makes it ",[1884,1953,1954],{},"slower",", the planner was right to seq-scan — the predicate isn't selective. If forcing makes it ",[1884,1957,1958],{},"faster",", the planner's stats are off — run ",[18,1961,1962],{},"ANALYZE events"," to refresh them.",[14,1965,1966],{},"Other possible causes:",[1458,1968,1969,1976,1987],{},[1461,1970,1971,1972,1975],{},"The table hasn't been ",[18,1973,1974],{},"ANALYZE","d since the index was created, so the planner doesn't know the index exists or has stale stats.",[1461,1977,1978,1979,1982,1983,1986],{},"The query is in a function with ",[18,1980,1981],{},"payload"," as a parameter, and the planner can't inline the expression (use ",[18,1984,1985],{},"IMMUTABLE"," expression or a generated column).",[1461,1988,1989,1990,1993,1994,1996],{},"The expression has a subtle mismatch (e.g., index on ",[18,1991,1992],{},"(payload ->> 'type')"," with different whitespace than the query's ",[18,1995,348],{}," — actually these are the same; the planner normalizes operator spacing).",[14,1998,1999,2002,2003,2006,2007,2010,2011,2013],{},[105,2000,2001],{},"The lesson",": an index that matches the query expression isn't always ",[1884,2004,2005],{},"used"," — the planner chooses based on cost. If the predicate isn't selective (most rows match), a seq scan is correct. Run ",[18,2008,2009],{},"EXPLAIN"," and, if needed, ",[18,2012,1974],{}," to refresh stats before assuming the index is broken.",[23,2015,2016],{"id":1869},"Summary",[14,2018,2019,2020,2022,2023,710,2025,710,2027,710,2029,2031,2032,710,2034,710,2036,2038,2039,710,2041,710,2043,2045],{},"You can now store and query semi-structured data with ",[18,2021,20],{}," (using ",[18,2024,324],{},[18,2026,338],{},[18,2028,381],{},[18,2030,404],{},"), index it with GIN or expression indexes, modify it with ",[18,2033,1696],{},[18,2035,902],{},[18,2037,933],{},", and use array columns with ",[18,2040,1763],{},[18,2042,381],{},[18,2044,1328],{}," — knowing when JSONB\u002Farrays are appropriate vs. when to normalize. Next: recursive CTEs for tree and graph traversal.",[2047,2048,2049],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .svdQ7, html code.shiki .svdQ7{--shiki-default:#D73A49;--shiki-github-dark:#F97583}html pre.shiki code .sIsaT, html code.shiki .sIsaT{--shiki-default:#6F42C1;--shiki-github-dark:#B392F0}html pre.shiki code .ssxIu, html code.shiki .ssxIu{--shiki-default:#24292E;--shiki-github-dark:#E1E4E8}html pre.shiki code .sdCPZ, html code.shiki .sdCPZ{--shiki-default:#6A737D;--shiki-github-dark:#6A737D}html pre.shiki code .sJ6F3, html code.shiki .sJ6F3{--shiki-default:#032F62;--shiki-github-dark:#9ECBFF}html .default .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .github-dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-github-dark);background: var(--shiki-github-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-github-dark-text-decoration);}html.github-dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-github-dark);background: var(--shiki-github-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-github-dark-text-decoration);}html pre.shiki code .snvgF, html code.shiki .snvgF{--shiki-default:#005CC5;--shiki-github-dark:#79B8FF}",{"title":127,"searchDepth":152,"depth":152,"links":2051},[2052,2053,2054,2059,2064,2065,2070,2071,2072,2073,2074],{"id":25,"depth":152,"text":26},{"id":116,"depth":152,"text":117},{"id":297,"depth":152,"text":298,"children":2055},[2056,2057],{"id":302,"depth":167,"text":303},{"id":603,"depth":167,"text":2058},"Containment @> is the indexable one",{"id":623,"depth":152,"text":624,"children":2060},[2061,2062,2063],{"id":627,"depth":167,"text":628},{"id":716,"depth":167,"text":717},{"id":786,"depth":167,"text":787},{"id":832,"depth":152,"text":833},{"id":980,"depth":152,"text":981,"children":2066},[2067,2068],{"id":1097,"depth":167,"text":1098},{"id":1325,"depth":167,"text":2069},"unnest — array to rows",{"id":1455,"depth":152,"text":1456},{"id":1483,"depth":152,"text":1484},{"id":1579,"depth":152,"text":1580},{"id":1794,"depth":152,"text":1795},{"id":1869,"depth":152,"text":2016},"PostgreSQL has first-class support for semi-structured data: JSONB (binary JSON) and array columns. This lets you store variable-structure data (event payloads, user preferences, nested objects) without a separate document database, while keeping relational power for the rest of the schema.","md",{},"\u002Fsql\u002F18-json-and-arrays",{"title":5,"description":2075},"sql\u002F18-json-and-arrays","4i4dP0rQ8fvz5biD2nSaDdgDdwWsQCyaw8-YaZZwing",1786943184623]